<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Westphalia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Westphalia is a region of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a>, centred on Münster and reaching south to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhr">Ruhr</a>. It was originally the western part of the land of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a>; after 1180 the title of Duke of Westphalia passed to the archbishops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, while most of the region was divided among bishoprics and counties. It became a Prussian province in 1815 and is now part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/westphalia.jpg" alt="Westphalia" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rüdiger Wölk · CC BY-SA 2.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MuensterPrinzipalmarkt09.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
